{
  "dom_id": "step-q161030",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "$$\nr(z,\\theta )=\u27e8{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,\\text{__________},\\,z\u27e9\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(z,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y={e}^{1-{z}^{2}}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [0,1].$ What is the second component of $r?$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071875/assets/04de4ea7dc617be1429c596812ce4f450c2cdaafd8d94d86f75e68a6743acba9.png)\n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(z)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis has the position vector \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\u27e8f(z)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,z\u27e9,\\,z\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(z)={e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [0,1].$  \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is  \n\n$$\nr(z,\\theta )=\u27e8{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,{e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,z\u27e9,\\,z\\in [0,1],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $r$ is ${e}^{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta .$",
